To protect against attacks by future , hybrid key agreement schemes have been deployed for TLS 1.3. These combine a classical exchange with , the lattice-based standardized by as FIPS 203 in August 2024. The TLS working group defines the hybrid groups X25519MLKEM768, SecP256r1MLKEM768 and SecP384r1MLKEM1024 in an Internet-Draft, alongside a general framework for hybrid key exchange in TLS 1.3 and a specification for standalone (non-hybrid) ML-KEM key agreement.To protect against "harvest now, decrypt later""harvest now, decrypt later" attacks by future quantum computersquantum computers, hybrid post-quantumpost-quantum key agreement schemes have been deployed for TLS 1.3. These combine a classical ECDHEECDHE exchange with ML-KEMML-KEM, the lattice-based key encapsulation mechanismkey encapsulation mechanism standardized by NISTNIST as FIPS 203 in August 2024.
